---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import Tree from "../../components/Tree.astro";

const CHART = "deploy/helm/iki";
const T = `${CHART}/templates`;

const H = {
  short: "What runs in a deployment's cluster",
  how: "One chart, templates rendered by value",
  rules: "The migrate hook, the network and where the ADR differs",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      One Helm chart for every environment, requiring Kubernetes 1.33 or later. It runs the web pods
      (which also serve MCP), the task runner, a migrate hook before every install, upgrade and
      rollback, an hourly maintenance job, and, as the values turn them on, backend workers, the
      provisioner for the container and VM tiers, the image policy and the egress proxy. Network
      policies that deny everything by default are on unless turned off (<Adr n={[14, 53, 155]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <Tree dir={T} depth={1} />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <Path p={`${T}/web-deployment.yaml`} />, with its Service, ingress, autoscaler and disruption
        budget: the web app, the API and MCP, which is a route on the web pods (<Path
          p="server/src/iki_server/urls.py"
          sym='path("mcp"'
        />).
      </li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/tasks-deployment.yaml`} />: background tasks from the Postgres queue, scaled out with replicas (<PageLink to="Tasks and live updates" />).</li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/maintenance-cronjob.yaml`} />: hourly housekeeping, such as expired sessions and tokens, and stored files past their retention.</li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/worker-deployment.yaml`} />: backend workers, with <code>backendWorkers.enabled</code>.</li>
      <li>
        <Path p={`${T}/provisioner.yaml`} />: the only part of Iki that talks to the Kubernetes API,
        when the container or VM tier is on (<PageLink to="Compute tiers" />).
      </li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/image-policy.yaml`} />: with the container tier and <code>imagePolicy.enabled</code> (<PageLink to="Releases and images" hash="rules" />).</li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/egress-proxy-deployment.yaml`} />: with <code>egressProxy.enabled</code>; until it's on, actions that declare hosts can't run (<PageLink to="Network and secrets" />).</li>
      <li><Path p={`${T}/monitoring.yaml`} />: Iki's Grafana dashboards and Prometheus alerts (<Adr n="0098" />).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The production roots of <PageLink to="Cloud infrastructure" /> each output the values the cloud
      decides, to sit beside a <code>values-production.yaml</code>.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The migrate hook" open>
        <p>
          A Helm hook Job runs before install, upgrade and rollback, before any new web pod starts. It
          migrates the database to this release's build as the schema's owner; on a rollback it
          reverses the plugin migrations the older build lacks, unless that could lose data (<Adr
            n="0034"
          />). The plugins' migrations, the triggers and grants they need, and the app role's grants
          are one transaction, so the old pods serving meanwhile never write to a table without its
          guards (<Adr n="0134" />). A failed step fails the install, upgrade or rollback, and the
          old pods keep serving (<Path p={`${T}/migrate-job.yaml`} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Network policies deny by default">
        <p>
          Iki's pods accept and send nothing except what the policy lists. DNS is always allowed;
          the peers that may reach the web pods, and where workers, run pods and the proxy may go,
          are values (<Adr n="0155" />, <Path p={`${T}/networkpolicy.yaml`} />). The dev cluster
          enforces them too (<PageLink to="The dev cluster" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADR and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0014" /> lists mcp and scheduler deployments; MCP is a route on the web pods, and
            scheduled work is the task runner and the maintenance CronJob. It also names IRSA (the
            code uses EKS Pod Identity) and MinIO (replaced by SeaweedFS in <Adr n="0022" />).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${CHART}/values.yaml`} />: every value, with what it does</li>
        <li><Path p={`${CHART}/Chart.yaml`} sym="kubeVersion" />: the Kubernetes it needs</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0014" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0034" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0053" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0134" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0155" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
